.TH CYAML_CLONE 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_clone \- produce a deep independent copy of a DOM subtree
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

cyaml cyaml_clone(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Copy node and all of its descendants.  The clone uses the same allocator
as the source tree.  It also carries over the tag of each node unchanged,
when the node has one (see cyaml_node_tag(3)), and every dictionary of the
clone keeps the member order of its source (see cyaml_dictionary_first(3)).
A change to the clone does
not affect the original.  A change to the original does not affect the
clone.

node need not come from cyaml_parse*().  A tree that the caller builds
directly with cyaml_create_list_mp(3) and cyaml_dictionary_set(3) obeys the
same 500-level nesting limit that cyaml_parse*(3) applies at parse time.
cyaml_serialize(3) and cyaml_serialize_flow(3) apply that same limit on
their own.  This function refuses a tree that is nested deeper than the
limit, and it returns NULL for such a tree.  It walks the source with an
explicit worklist, not by recursion.  The call stack that it needs is
therefore a constant that does not grow with the nesting depth.  The limit
bounds the work of one call, not the stack that the caller must give.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  Root of the subtree to copy.  The function accepts NULL and then
      returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Root of the new tree, which the caller owns.  The function returns NULL
when it runs out of memory, and also when node is nested more than 500
levels deep.  The caller cannot tell the two causes apart, because this
function has no separate error-string channel.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_destroy (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cyaml_node_tag (3)
